---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import ScheduleScreen from '../../components/ScheduleScreen.astro';
import { monthMarkupSample } from '../../lib/schedule-data';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: schedule">
  <p class="demo-note">
    A planner or supervisor scans a month for what is booked — deliveries,
    maintenance windows, shift cover — several times a day during a busy
    week, and once a day otherwise. "Done" is picking a day and seeing
    exactly what is on it without losing the month underneath, the same
    swap discipline <a href={base + '/patterns/master-detail'}>master-detail</a>
    uses for a list and a record. <strong>Not for authoring a schedule</strong> —
    creating, moving or cancelling entries is the admin module's job, not this
    screen's — and <strong>not for one entry's own detail or history</strong>,
    which belongs to <a href={base + '/patterns/record-detail'}>record
    detail</a>, not a month grid. This page only answers "what is on this
    day".
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The screen</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Sized to this page's column below — the
    real screen is full-width. <a href={base + '/patterns/schedule/full'}
    target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it full screen ↗</a> (new tab, no
    docs chrome — the same markup below, at real scale).</p>
    <div class="sched-split">
      <ScheduleScreen />
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      The month stays put while the day-detail list changes — the calendar
      is never re-rendered on a click, so the reader's place in the month
      survives. Only days carrying at least one entry are real links;
      an empty workday is a plain cell, the same "not everything is
      clickable" discipline the calendar component's own picker uses.
    </p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>Which day is open is carried the same way master-detail carries
      row selection</strong> — <code>data-day="selected"</code> on the cell,
      which is the calendar component's own state, reused rather than
      invented. There is no second "which day is open" mechanism to keep in
      sync with it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Month grid</strong> — <a href={base + '/components/calendar'}>calendar</a>;
        a day with entries is a real link, a day with none is a plain cell.</li>
      <li><strong>Which days carry work</strong> — the calendar's own
        <code>data-day</code> marks (<code>closed</code>, <code>holiday</code>,
        <code>today</code>) plus whether the cell is a link at all; no new
        attribute for "has entries", the link/no-link distinction already carries it.</li>
      <li><strong>Open-day indicator</strong> — <code>data-day="selected"</code>
        on the chosen cell, the same value the calendar's picker already ships.</li>
      <li><strong>Day-detail panel</strong> — a <a href={base + '/components/dashboard#card'}>card</a>
        beside the month, holding a compact <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>data table</a>
        of that day's entries with a status <a href={base + '/components/badge'}>badge</a> per row.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Returns / on failure</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /schedule?month=2026-08</td><td>the month grid, with each day's <code>data-day</code> mark and whether it is a link baked in server-side; the detail panel renders empty until a day is chosen</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /schedule/day/:date</td><td><strong>the day-detail fragment only</strong>, swapped into <code>#schedule-detail</code>. Never re-render the month — that is what loses the reader's place in it</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">404 / day now empty</td><td>an entry was cancelled after the month rendered — replace the panel with an empty state naming the day, and stop treating that cell as a link on the next month fetch</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">GET /schedule/day/:date on a closed or holiday date</td><td>never happens from a click (those cells are plain spans, not links); a bookmarked URL still gets a real answer: an empty state saying the works calendar has no entries possible that day</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>No AI-manages/human-monitors split
    here, stated rather than left silent</strong>: this screen is a planner
    reading and booking dates, not oversight of an automated process — the
    closed/holiday marks are static business rules, not something an agent
    manages behind the scenes. There is no monitoring signal to name.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Nothing selected</td><td>the panel holds a one-line prompt ("Pick a day to see what's on it"), not a blank box</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Loading the day</td><td><code>aria-busy</code> on the panel only; the month stays interactive so the reader can pick a different day mid-load</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Empty day</td><td>a day that IS a link (server said it has entries) but returns none by the time it's opened — say so, do not show an empty table with headers and nothing under them</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Non-working day opened via a bookmark</td><td>the DIFFERENT empty — explain it is a closed/holiday date, not "nothing scheduled yet"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Conflict on one entry</td><td>flagged in the row with a badge (see the demo above), not surfaced as a page-level error — the other entries that day are still fine</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No permission to open a day's detail</td><td>the day stays a plain cell rather than a disabled-looking link — a control a reader can see but never use is worse than one that is simply absent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Narrow screen</td><td>panel and grid stack in source order — the split's own <code>auto-fit</code> grid (see <code>.sched-split</code> below) collapses to one column below ~36rem; the calendar component itself carries no container breakpoint, nothing new to build here</td></tr>
          <tr><td>No JS</td><td>fully functional — this page has no JS dependency; each day is a real <code>&lt;a href="#schedule-detail"&gt;</code> fragment link (native anchor navigation, no script), and the page imports no <code>@busy-office/ui/js</code> behavior</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 2 of 4</span></p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/calendar'}>Calendar</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/dashboard#card'}>Card</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/data-table'}>Data table</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/badge'}>Badge</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{monthMarkupSample}</code></pre>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ["/components/calendar", "Calendar"],
    ["/patterns/master-detail", "Master-detail (the swap-contract precedent)"],
    ["/components/data-table", "Data table"],
    ["/concepts/concurrency", "Concurrency & conflicts"],
  ]} />
</Gallery>

<style>
  /* Same reasoning as /patterns/master-detail's .md-split: a plain bordered
     auto-fit grid, not .bo-app-shell (100dvh, wrong for an embedded demo) and
     not .bo-grid (auto-fill keeps empty tracks, which would shrink both panes
     at wide widths to make room for columns holding nothing). */
  .sched-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(18rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--bo-space-4);
    padding: var(--bo-space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-color-border-default);
    border-radius: var(--bo-radius-md);
  }
</style>
